From 842d4acc7428035835fdb422d857d700b30db4ef Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 22:25:11 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] lightningd: say why an invstring was rejected in
 listinvoices

`listinvoices` discards the reason a string failed to decode and answers
"Invalid invstring", which tells a user who pasted an invoice for the other
rule set nothing they can act on. pay.c has carried the reason all along.

It matters more once option_blake2b is what separates invoices, because the
reason is then "9: unknown feature bit 512" rather than anything a reader could
infer from looking at the string.
---
 lightningd/invoice.c | 10 +++++++++-
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/lightningd/invoice.c b/lightningd/invoice.c
index 051c23472..61420ffd3 100644
--- a/lightningd/invoice.c
+++ b/lightningd/invoice.c
@@ -1378,8 +1378,16 @@ static struct command_result *json_listinvoices(struct command *cmd,
 						 cmd->ld->our_features, NULL,
 						 &fail);
 			if (!b12 || !b12->invoice_payment_hash) {
+				/* Carry the reason. "Invalid invstring" on
+				 * its own tells a user who pasted an invoice
+				 * for the other rule set nothing at all, and
+				 * under option_blake2b the reason is a
+				 * feature-bit message they would never guess.
+				 * pay.c has always done this. */
 				return command_fail(cmd, JSONRPC2_INVALID_PARAMS,
-						    "Invalid invstring");
+						    "Invalid invstring: %s",
+						    fail ? fail
+						    : "not bolt11 or bolt12");
 			}
 			payment_hash = b12->invoice_payment_hash;
 		}
